# L5R 5E - COURTS OF STONE: THE CRANE CLAN (LORE)

**Version:** 0.4
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game, 5th Edition - Courts of Stone (Chapter 1)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## THE CRANE CLAN

When the Kami Doji arrived in the Mortal Realm, she found fragmented coastal domains warring over borders. She became fascinated by human cultures and despaired at the violence. She codified the cultures she found most excellent, creating a common written and spoken language over nearly a decade, laying the groundwork for the bureaucracy that would allow the Emerald Empire to flourish.

The descendants of Doji's followers consider themselves wardens of Rokugan's cultural heritage. The Crane seek to elevate culture and fulfill Lady Doji's vision of beauty, order, and civility. Known as the Emperor's Left Hand, they are the stewards of the Throne's political power and the architects of many courtly institutions. Although their influence has waned following a catastrophic tsunami, the Crane remain one of the primary political powers.

---

### The Cost of Grace

Many arts, languages, and cultures that did not become threads in Doji's tapestry were driven to the wayside. Some divergent traditions persisted (the Isawa from the Dawn Peaks, the Asako's venerable ways), but others were lost outright. In the year 451, an anonymous treatise called The Cost of Grace challenged Doji's descendants and Doji herself for the destruction of vast beauty and knowledge. It was banned within a month but copies still surface. Some Crane scholars have been influenced by it, seeking to understand pre-Kami cultures.

---

### The Year Scroll

Honored guests of Crane households may notice a scroll displayed prominently. At the start of the year it is blank. As weeks pass, family members chronicle significant events in poems, paintings, or prose. On the last day of each year, the family reads the scroll together, then burns it at dawn on the first day of the new year, reminding themselves of impermanence and recommitting to live fully and presently.

---

## FAMILIES OF THE CRANE

The Kakita embody spirit and pride, earning glory with good works. The Daidoji serve as tireless guardians. The Asahina tend to the clan's very soul as shugenja. The Doji unite the families as voice and guiding hand. Like the spokes of an unfolding fan, these four families are the strength behind the beauty of the Crane.

---

### Doji -- The Noble Heart

**Origin:** Lady Doji was discovered on a beach by fishers and craftspeople. She embraced their customs, and her grace won their adoration. When neighboring communities united to claim her by force, Doji stepped forth and none could raise a weapon against her. Her words pierced hearts as surely as arrows. Tempers calmed and conflicts ended. From this came the banner of the Crane.

A mysterious beggar appeared during a heavy snow and helped Doji understand the beauty inherent in impermanence. She resolved to live keenly aware of each passing moment and to transcend mortality through appreciation of beauty.

**Reputation and Values:** The Doji stand at the center of high culture. Their network of friends and allies is larger than any other family's. They accept short-term losses if it means an enemy can become an ally. Members guard personal reputation to the point of obsession; death may be preferable to significant loss of face. They find natural rivals in the Bayushi and compete with the Akodo for the Emperor's favor. Their staunchest allies are the Shinjo, owing to the bond between Doji and Shinjo as siblings.

**Culture:** Preoccupied with propriety. Everything has a proper method, even pouring tea. Every Doji is expected to be highly educated, weaving references to history, literature, and arts seamlessly into conversation. Layered meaning pervades all interactions: colors of outfits evoke specific feelings, serving ochazuke signals the end of a gathering. Messages are encoded into art and song, comprehended only by the intended few.

**Speaking Without Words:** The Doji master the art of wordplay, using implication and silence as effectively as words. Hanakotoba (the language of flowers) encodes specific messages through bloom selection, color, size, and placement. Cadence is a secret Crane code of gestures, colors, and poetic words that allows even placing a tea ladle or holding a fan to convey hidden meaning. Never taught outside the Crane.

**Family Traditions:** Newborns do not receive names until their fifth year. Gifts for newborns are declined until then. The eldest child of a Doji couple never marries out of the clan. Honorable retirement is considered a reward, not something dreaded. Many retired Doji join the Brotherhood of Shinsei or become monks devoted to Benten. Some officially retired Doji secretly remain in service as spies.

**Schools:** The Doji maintain several schools. All curricula incorporate oratory and artistic skill. The Doji Bureaucrat School demands flawless calligraphy. The Doji Rhetorician masters poetic metaphor. The Inner Court Guard (elite bushi-courtiers) protect the Emperor's spouse. The Doji Academy at Shizuka Toshi is the clan's premier courtier training center.

**Recent Concerns:** Natural disasters and an unexpected tsunami have damaged coastal villages. Military struggles against the Lion drain resources. The Crane's coffers are running dry, but the Doji refuse to admit weakness. The Bayushi have claimed important positions near the aging Emperor that were traditionally Crane. Emerald Champion Doji Satsume died under mysterious circumstances. Champion Doji Hotaru tasks the clan with finding allies against Lion and Scorpion.

---

### Kakita -- The Brilliant Blade

**Origin:** At the first Emerald Championship, Matsu swept every opponent aside. In the final round she faced an unknown swordsman who fought in a strange style, drawing and striking in one motion. In a single strike the duel was over, the stranger victorious. He refused to bow to Matsu, as she had shown no courtesy to those she defeated. She demanded his name. He softly replied, "My name is Kakita." This sparked the longest-running blood feud in Rokugan's history.

**Reputation and Values:** The Kakita pursue mastery over life itself, believing complete devotion to an art embodies excellence in all things. They see passions as assets, not something to be ashamed of. They are the clan's functional assassins, trapping targets in duels of honor and provoking opponents with slights, then slaying them publicly and being praised for their skill.

**Culture:** Excellence is the driving concept. Kakita are expected to devote their entire life to mastering an art or skill. Their greatest rivals are the Mirumoto family of the Dragon, whose two-sword style Kakita himself once called "The Coward's Way." A mutual if grudging respect underlies their centuries-long rivalry.

**Kenshinzen:** The most elite Kakita warriors. Only those who defeat an existing kenshinzen in honorable duel may join. Only a handful exist at any time. The head of the dueling academy is always a member.

**Master Artisans:** Those who fully comprehend the secret of surrendering a piece of themselves into their work. With each masterwork they grow physically and spiritually weaker, imbuing each creation with their very essence. These rare artifacts are known to transfix the kami themselves.

**Yasurugi and Konishiko:** Lady Doji's eldest children. Yasurugi forged five exquisite blades for a tengu, including Shukujo (ancestral sword of the Crane) and Kunshu (ancestral sword of the Hantei). Konishiko, though she had never studied the sword, picked up Shukujo and volunteered as the Crane Clan Thunder when Yasurugi was slain.

**The Heart of Doji:** Kakita fell in love with Lady Doji. She gave him three impossible tasks. His twin sister Kiyamori told stories of Kakita's nature to Doji, who fell in love. Kakita brought dead driftwood to life with a biwa, argued the sun-guided walk takes only a day, and held a mirror to show Doji her own reflection as unmatchable beauty.

**Kyuden Kakita Curse:** When a child is born within the palace while the gates are closed, should that child ever draw steel, the blades of the Kakita will turn against their masters. The current daimyo Kakita Yoshi was born during a Lion siege when the gates were closed. He followed the path of the courtier and refuses to touch a blade.

---

### Daidoji -- The Silent Guardians

**Origin:** Doji Hayaku, Lady Doji's youngest son, embarked alone into the Shadowlands to find his fallen sister Konishiko. He returned after three years carrying Shukujo, untainted but changed -- scarred, voiceless, his raven hair turned white. He was made daimyo and charged with the Crane's defense. They called him Daidoji ("Defender of the Doji").

**Reputation and Values:** Dour where Doji are sunny, quiet where Kakita are boastful. They eat only with the left hand so the right is free to draw a blade. They see cleverness, watchfulness, and determination as virtues. They emphasize Duty and Loyalty (Chugi) above all. They accept losses without hesitating, willing to "sacrifice a finger to save the hand," but draw the line at dishonor.

**Culture:** Every waking moment spent in productive activity. Children learn shogi, Go, and box puzzles. Instead of folktales, they read strategy and tactics. Poetry is martial training for the spontaneous mind. They prefer the shakuhachi (bamboo flute) as it is easily carried. Many dye their hair white in honor of Hayaku, and some leaders take vows of silence.

**Warfare:** Crane forces are among the least numerous in the Empire. The Daidoji fight defensively and stubbornly, buying time for political intervention. Scouts covertly follow armies. They use terrain, smoke, kites for signaling, and resource denial (burning supplies rather than letting them fall to enemies). If enemies truly committed to full-scale invasion, Crane forces would crumple. Daidoji warfare is fundamentally stalling tactics while the political machine works.

**The Iron Crane (Iron Warriors):** The most prestigious Daidoji warriors. They spend at least one year on the Carpenter Wall alongside the Crab. They tattoo crane images on their wrists or forearms, adding designs for victories and hardships. Close friends are tattooed in tandem with designs completed only when clasping hands.

**Kuranoko's Feathers:** Publicly a cartography and merchant-trade school, secretly a smuggling organization that bypasses blockades to deliver medicine, food, artifacts, and people. Without them, the distant Crane territories would never survive.

**The Yasuki Grudge:** When the Yasuki defected to the Crab Clan in the fourth century, the resulting war was so heated the Emperor had to intervene. The Daidoji have never forgiven the betrayal and seek to undermine the Yasuki whenever possible.

**The Crane's Feathers:** Many Crane bleach their hair white in honor of Hayaku. Blue eyes (said to be Doji's color) are a not-uncommon feature. Very rarely, a Crane is born with naturally white hair, seen as an auspicious mark.

**The Night of Falling Stars:** Over a century and a half ago, the Lion overran Yojin no Shiro. Rather than surrender, the Crane occupants -- courtiers, retainers, even children -- threw themselves from the towers clutching lanterns, appearing as falling stars. The vengeful Daidoji Yurei spent his life trying to retake the castle but never succeeded. His ghost is said to haunt the gates.

---

### Asahina -- The Peacemakers

**Origin:** In the fourth century, Isawa Asahina, the Elemental Master of Fire, was furious that the Crane had brokered peace between Phoenix and Lion. He rampaged through Crane lands, setting villages ablaze. When Doji Kiriko begged him to spare her village and he razed it anyway, seeing her grief moved him to compassion. He forswore violence and devoted himself to repairing lives, promising his descendants would never embrace violence again.

**Reputation and Values:** Reclusive, rarely leaving their temples in the southern provinces. Completely devoted pacifists who refuse even self-defense. Masters of Fu Sui (geomancy). Known for their tsangusuri, the creation of tsang (items with temporarily awakened kami), providing miraculous but time-limited powers. Their ephemeral beauty leads other shugenja to regard them as prideful.

**Culture:** Lives spent in quiet contemplation and veneration of the kami. Some wander Crane lands blessing crops, treating illness, invoking weather, and entreating kami for the benefit of all. When forced to engage in violence (such as against Tainted beings), they undertake far deeper spiritual purifications afterward.

**Tsangusuri:** The art of awakening the innate spirit dwelling within an object. Each spirit has two aspects: shen (benevolent) and kuei (wrathful). The Asahina bring out only the benevolent side. Not to be confused with Unicorn meishōdō, which binds a spirit to an object rather than awakening the object's innate spirit.

**Asahina Yajinden:** In the fifth century, a brilliant smith was approached by a mysterious stranger who helped him unlock new techniques. The resulting artifacts drove their wielders to madness and violence. Yajinden tried to forge a corrective blade but died before completing it. The Asahina sealed his works beneath Shinden Asahina temple and removed his name from genealogies. Unknown numbers of his cursed works remain at large.

---

## CRANE LANDS

The most geographically diverse in Rokugan. Verdant northern fields, temperate forests, marshy southern wetlands. Crane lands account for seventy percent of Rokugan's coastline. Abundant rice paddies, fishing villages, orchards, and marketplaces make them the breadbasket of the Empire.

---

### Northern and Southern Provinces

Northern provinces are the seat of power. Kyuden Doji and Kyuden Kakita are a day's ride from Otosan Uchi. Southern provinces are less developed and wilder, with troublesome samurai assigned there as punishment. Passing Crane on roads north and south are advised to treat each other well, as one never knows when the trip might be reversed.

---

### Shizuka Toshi (The Quiet City)

Home of the Doji Academy, nestled in the seclusion of the Osari Mori forest. Students begin training around age ten. Outsiders are rarely permitted. Whatever happens within never leaves the boundaries of the forest. Shizuka Palace, modeled after Kyuden Doji, contains classrooms, dormitories, libraries, and a replica Imperial Court Chamber. Permanent station there is considered career death for ordinary samurai.

---

### The Eye of the Needle

A tiny sacred forest near Kyuden Doji. When Prince Hantei XXIII ran away as a child, a silver fox led him to a hidden grove and kept him safe. As Emperor, he declared the forest sacred. Whenever the moon is full for the third time within the same season, barriers between the forest and Spirit Realms grow weak, allowing overlap with Ningen-do. The Doji leave gifts at the forest border on these occasions.

---

### River of the Blind Monk

Named for a mysterious figure who appears to lone travelers. The blind monk ferries anyone who can answer his riddle but tosses those who offer payment into the water. Sightings are recorded by famous samurai in Crane histories, but the monk's true nature remains undetermined.

---

### Motsureta Mura (Tangled Path Village)

In the Uebe Marshes of the southern provinces. Named for confusing and often-flooded roads, but "motsureta" also means "confused" and "embarrassed." Assignment there is perceived as punishment. The abandoned sector is said to be inhabited by kawauso (shapeshifting river otters). Crossing the lantern bridge, one is supposed to close one's eyes until reaching solid ground for good fortune. The Laughing River Otter ryokan has an onsen said to be good for one's complexion.

---

### Musume Mura (Daughter Village)

Near Kyuden Doji. Contains the Doji's most prestigious nakodo (matchmaker) school. Samurai parents make pilgrimages for matchmaking advice. When the Emperor becomes engaged, their intended spends a night alone in the shrine on Lost Daughter Inlet, receiving the blessing of the first Imperial Spouse.

---

### The Great Sea Spider

At the center of an atoll off the Daidoji coast churns a dark whirlpool. Legends speak of Daikumo-no-Kami, the Great Sea Spider, bound there by Asahina shugenja. Kumo (shape-changing spider spirits) feed on negative emotions. The Great Sea Spider bound itself to the Daidoji family, feeding on their anger and vengeance. The regional saying "to feed the spider" means harboring destructive negative emotions.

---

### The Night of Falling Stars

Yojin no Shiro, just north of Kyuden Kakita, was seized by the Lion over 150 years ago. The Crane within chose mass suicide over surrender, clutching lanterns as they fell from the towers. Daidoji Yurei, born on the battlefield that night, spent his life wearing ghostly white armor trying to retake the castle but never succeeded. His spirit is said to haunt the gates, and on the anniversary, ghosts of the falling Doji can be seen trailing fire.

---

### Uebe Beach

The singing sands near Motsureta Mura whistle in the wind and make a striking sound when stepped upon. Spirits beneath the sand are said to be trying to recall a princess's beautiful song. A shrine to Isora, Fortune of the Shore, rises just offshore.
